Important overlap: K6.3a conflict markers in entry files — memory_index flags them; but index conflict only via K6.2. Fine.

Also K6.1a/K6.2 duplicates: they look the same check. K6.1a "INDEX.md is generated from entries: one line per active entry, showing id/kind/claim" and fails_if "differs from what the tool builds ... or lists a superseded or retired entry as active". K6.2 "regenerated and committed in the same commit as the entry" and fails_if same "a commit's INDEX.md differs from what the tool builds from that commit's entries." These are nearly identical fails_if! K6.1a is about content, K6.2 about same-commit commit. Both are satisfied by `--check <rev>` for each commit. But checking every commit is expensive. K6.2 "in the same commit as the entry" is stronger: need per-commit check, not just HEAD. `--check <rev>` for each commit in history. If only HEAD is checked, an earlier inconsistent commit still violates K6.2. Docs don't say the range. Concrete: C1/C3 say check K6.1a/K6.2 but not over what history. Recommendation: default to HEAD (and staged) for the warning, with `--all-commits`/`--since` for a full report; say which. Or, if the goal is "the next session sees it", HEAD is what matters. But K6.2's text says "a commit's INDEX.md"; so full history is the literal check. This is a cost/scope decision for EJ.

