Now git history cost: default HEAD + staged vs all commits. I'd recommend: the command checks the current snapshot (HEAD or working tree?) and the *last commit* for the commit-together (K6.2) because "a commit's INDEX differs" literally applies to every commit, and checking full history is the only honest reading. But cost on a big repo: `git rev-list --all -- project-memory/entries | while read c; do memory_index --check c` is O(commits) python spawns — way too slow for session start. Better: a single Python `--check <rev>` per commit is too slow. Alternative: check HEAD only and report K6.2 as "pass at HEAD; earlier commits not checked" — but then "a commit's" isn't fully checked. C1 says checkable requirements checked. Hmm.

Actually, one can check K6.2 across history more cheaply: `git log --format=%H -- project-memory/entries project-memory/INDEX.md` then for each commit, verify the INDEX blob equals `render_index(entries at that commit)` — memory_index.py --check does exactly that per commit, needing a process per commit. For a memory folder with a few hundred commits that's fine at report time (a full report on demand), not at session start. So: report mode = full history (or `--since`); warn mode = HEAD + staged only. This matches "full report on demand, warnings at session start" (VISION:5-6). That's a clean design: two modes with different depth. I can recommend that. It also gives the warning a session-fast budget. Cost: the warning may miss an old bad commit; it tells the agent about current memory, which is what matters at start. Good.

Snapshot: for the warning, "current" should be HEAD or working tree? The agent starts work on the working tree it has. If the working tree has uncommitted broken memory (mid-edit), warning could be noise. K6's requirement is per-commit, so HEAD is the consistent snapshot; checking working-tree entries against HEAD's INDEX would flag uncommitted edits as K6 failures before they're staged — perhaps correct K6.2? K6.2 says "whenever an entry is created or changed, INDEX is regenerated and committed in the same commit" — an unstaged edit is not a commit; the requirement doesn't apply until commit. So warning on HEAD avoids false K6 fails. But then a broken unstaged entry file isn't warned about. The agent is about to work; maybe fine. Recommendation: default snapshot = HEAD (committed memory), `--staged` and `--worktree` opt-in; report states which. This is a "what I need to know" because EJ may want the working tree. But I can recommend HEAD with reasons and ask.

